Step 1: The common point of tangency must lie on the line that connects the centers of the two original circles. This line is the x-axis. So, the third circle must pass through the point $(0,5).$

Step 2: Let the x and y coordinates of the point of tangency be ($t,0$). Since the circle with center $(4,0)$ is tangent to the third circle, the distance between the two centers is the sum of the radii:
\begin{equation*}
4 + 1 = (-t - 4) + \sqrt{(4-t)^2 + 0^2} + 1.
\end{equation*}\
Expanding and getting the equation of the circle centered at $(-4,0)$ that is tangent to the third circle:
\begin{align*}
(4 + 1 + (-t - 4) + 1)^2 &= (4 - t)^2 + 1^2 \\
9 + 8t + t^2 &= 16 - 8t + t^2 + 1 \\
2t^2 + 0t + 17 &= 0.
\end{align*}
Note that this equation has no real solutions, so no circles are tangent to both given circles and pass through the point $(0,5).$

\boxed{No real solutions}

