 \begin{document}

Let $n$ be a natural number with exactly 2 positive prime divisors. If $n^2$ has 27 divisors, how many does $n$ have?

1. Since $n^2$ has 27 divisors, then by Euler's totient theorem, $n$ has $\phi(n) = n \pointdot(n - 1)$ divisors. We know that $\phi(n)$ is even since $n$ has only 2 prime factors, assuming they are distinct. Let their product be $k$ and let one of the prime factors be $p$. Then,
\begin{align*}
kn & = n^2 \cdot \phi(p) \cdot \phi(k/p) \\
& = n^2 \cdot (p - 1) \cdot \frac{k - p}{p - 1}
\end{align*}

2. The equality $kn = n^2 \cdot (p - 1) \cdot \frac{k - p}{p - 1}$ now implies that $p \cdot (k - p) = k^2 - n^2$.

3. Since $p$ and $k$ are integers, $p$ and $k - p$ must have a common factor. Thus, $p$ must be a factor of $k^2 - n^2$.

4. Let $k = p\cdot q$, where $q$ is another integer. Then,
\begin{align*}
p^2q^2 & = p^2 \cdot (q^2 - n^2) \\
q^2 & = q^2 - n^2 \tag{1}
\end{align*}
Equation (1) has no positive integer solutions for $q^2$, since if $q^2 = t$ is a positive integer, then $t - n^2 < t$, contradicting the fact that $q$ is a positive integer (since it is the square of an integer).

5. Because we cannot find any integer solution for $q^2$, $k$ and hence $n$ must not have exactly 2 prime factors. Contradiction.

