In total, there are $20$ members, so the number of ways to select one president is $20$. To select a vice-president, we have $19$ members left. For the treasurer, we have $18$ members remaining. Using the principle of counting without repetition, the number of combinations is given by the factorial of the number of members minus thefactorials of the number of members for each office. Therefore,
\begin{align*}
\text{{Number of combinations}} &= \frac{20!}{1!19!} \times \frac{19!}{1!18!} \times \frac{18!}{1!17!}\\
&= \frac{20 \times 19 \times 18!}{1 \times 19! \times 1 \times 18! \times 17!}
\end{align*}
Now we simplify the fraction:
\begin{align*}
&= \frac{20 \times 19}{1 \times 17}\\
&= \boxed{\frac{380}{17}}
\end{align*}
No units are required for this problem, as the answer is purely numerical.

