To solve this problem we can use the property that the ratio of corresponding sides is equal in similar triangles. Let us denote the ratio of the corresponding sides as $r$:

\begin{aligned}
  r &= \frac{AB}{XY} \\
  r &= \frac{BC}{YZ} \\
  r &= \frac{AC}{ZX}
\end{aligned}

Using the given measurements, we have:

\begin{aligned}
  r &= \frac{4}{14} \\
  r &= \frac{6}{Y^2} \\
  r &= \frac{c}{10}
\end{aligned}

where $c$ represents the unknown length of side $ZX$.

From the first and third equations, we can find $BC$ and $AC$ in terms of $YZ$:

\begin{aligned}
  \frac{AB}{XY} &= \frac{BC}{YZ} \\
  4 \cdot 10 &= 6 \cdot YZ \\
  YZ &= \frac{40}{6} \\
  YZ &= \frac{10}{3}
\end{aligned}

\begin{aligned}
  \frac{AB}{XY} &= \frac{AC}{ZX} \\
  4 \cdot 10 &= c \cdot YZ \\
  c &= \frac{40}{YZ} \\
  c &= 15 \frac{2}{3}
\end{aligned}

Since triangle $ABC$ is similar to triangle $XYZ$, the corresponding angles have the same measure, so angle $ZXC$ must be equal to angle $YZX$. The sum of the angles inside a triangle is $180^\circ$, so we can use that to find angle $ZXC$:

\begin{aligned}
  \angle A + \angle B + \angle C &= 180^\circ \\
  \angle Z + \angle X + \angle Y + \angle X + \angle Z + \angle C &= 180^\circ \\
  90^\circ + 90^\circ + \angle Y + 90^\circ + \angle Y + \angle C &= 180^\circ \\
  2 \angle Y + \angle C &= 90^\circ \\
  \angle Y &= \frac{90^\circ - \angle C}{2} \\
  \angle Y &= \frac{90^\circ - 90^\circ}{18} = \frac{10^\circ}{3}
\end{aligned}

Now we can find the length of side $ZX$:

\begin{aligned}
  r &= \frac{AC}{ZX} \\
  15 \frac{2}{3} &= \frac{c}{ZX} \\
  ZX &= \frac{30}{3} \\
  ZX &= 10
\end{aligned}

And since opposite sides are equal in similar triangles, side $YZ$ also has a length of $10$.

\boxed{10}

