The general strategy for this problem is to interpret the equation as a system of two trigonometric equations in terms of $\theta$. We know that the sine and cosine functions are periodic with periods of $2\pi$ and $4\pi$, respectively. Hence, we can rescaling our angle as $\theta \rightarrow \theta + n\pi$ or $\theta \rightarrow \theta + 2m\pi$ for integers $n$ and $m$, and check which scalings give us an actual solution.

\begin{align}
\sin 3\theta &= \cos 7\theta \\
\sin(3\theta + 2\pi k) &= \cos(7\theta + 4\pi l) & \text{ for integers $k, l$}
\end{align}

First, let's notice that $\sin(\theta) = \cos((\pi/2) - \theta)$ and $\cos(\theta) = \sin((\pi/2) - \theta)$. This means we have the following equivalent system of equations:

\begin{align}
\sin(3\theta + 3\pi k) &= \cos(7\theta + 7\pi l) \\
\sin((3\theta + 3\pi k) - \pi/2) &= \cos((7\theta + 7\pi l) - \pi/2) \\
\Leftrightarrow \cos(3\theta + 3\pi k) &= -\sin(7\theta + 7\pi l)
\end{align}

Now let's try solving this system graphically using the unit circle:

