\draw [green, thick] plot[smooth] coordinates {(0,0) (60,.5) (300,.5) (350,0.05) (7.14,1.2989) (6.7,-.9) (2.8,-.26) (1.5,-.5)};
\end{tikzpicture}
\caption{The graph of the functions $\sin(3\theta)$ and $\cos(7\theta)$ along with two solutions that intersect at approximately $(\frac{2\pi}{9},\frac{\sqrt{3}-\sqrt{2}}{8})$.}
\end{figure}

The graphically we see the solution lies between the angles of $60$ degrees and $300$ degrees, but we need to find the precise intersection point.

We can express the intersection point $\langle x,y \rangle$ through the system of equations:

\begin{align}
\sin{ 3x } &= y = \cos{ 7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) } \\
\sin{ 3x }^2 + y^2 &= 1 \\
y^2 &= 1 - \sin^2(3x) \\
y &= \pm \sqrt{1 - \sin^2(3x)}
\end{align}

Notice that the radicand is always positive, meaning the solutions for $y$ are both real-valued, and as such there is exactly one solution for $y$.

Now we can check its value:

\begin{align}
y &= \pm \sqrt{1 - \sin^2(3x)} \\
\sin(3x) &= y = \cos(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) ) \\
\Leftrightarrow & \\
1 - y^2 &= \sin^2(3x) \\
1 - y^2 &= \cos^2(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) ) \\
\sin^2(3x) &= \cos^2(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) ) \\
\sin(3x) &= \pm \cos(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) )
\end{align}

From our graph, it is clear that $\sin(3x)$ and $\cos(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) )$ have the same sign, and we will assume that they are both positive for simplicity:

\begin{align}
\sin(3x) &= \cos(7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) ) \\
\Leftrightarrow 3x &= 7(x-\tfrac{2\pi}{9}) + \arccos(y) \\
\Leftrightarrow 9x &= 2\pi \left( \tfrac{4}{9} + x - \tfrac{2\pi}{36} \right) + \arccos(y) \\
\Leftrightarrow y &= \sqrt{1 - \sin^2(3x)} \\
\Leftrightarrow 0 &= \sin^2(3x) - y^2
\end{align}

Finally, we solve for the $x$ value:

\begin{align}
\sin^2(3x) &= y^2 \\
\sin^2(3x) &= (\sqrt{1 - \sin^2(3x)})^2 \\
\Leftrightarrow 2\sin(3x) \cos^2(3x) &= 0 \\
\Leftrightarrow \sin(3x) &= 0 \\
\Leftrightarrow 3x &= 2\pi n \\
\end{align}

where $n$ is an integer:

\begin{align}
x &= \tfrac {2\pi}{9} n
\end{align}

So the smallest positive angle $\theta$ is $\theta = \boxed{\tfrac{2\pi}{9}}$.

