First, calculate the original volume of the cube:
\begin{align*}
\text{original volume } &= \text{length}^3 \text{ (cube formula)} \\
&\text{ (since it is a 2-cm-by-2cm-by-2cm cube: )} \\
&= (2\text{ cm})^3 \\
&= 8\text{ cubic centimeters }
\end{align*}

Add the increase in volume:
\begin{align*}
\text{new volume } &= \text{original volume } + 19\text{ cubic centimeters } \\
&= (8\text{ cubic centimeters}) + (19\text{ cubic centimeters}) \\
&= 27\text{ cubic centimeters }
\end{align*}

Find the side length of the new cube:
\begin{align*}
\text{new side length }^3 &= \text{new volume} \\
\sqrt[3]{\text{new side length}^3} &= \sqrt[3]{\text{new volume}} \\
\text{new side length } &= \sqrt[3]{\text{new volume}}
\end{align*}

\boxed{ \text{length} = \sqrt[3]{27\text{ cubic centimeters}} }

